From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: iasnezhkov <iasnezhkov@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 05:26:47 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 27/27] v4l2: downconvert 10-bit ByteBuffer decode output to
 NV12

c2.v4l2.hevc.decoder decoding HEVC Main10 into a ByteBuffer (MediaCodec
configured without a surface, client did not request
COLOR_FormatYUVP010) fails with "sendOutputBuffers: tried 5 realloc and
failed". CCodec silently defaults an unrequested
ByteBuffer output to 8-bit YUV420, but DecodeInterface declared no
C2StreamPixelFormatInfo at all, so the client had nothing to negotiate
against: V4L2Decoder always allocated a 10-bit P010 block whenever the
V4L2 capture format was 10-bit (V4L2Decoder.cpp picks
HalPixelFormat::YCBCR_P010 for P010/MTST/MTSR), and
GraphicView2MediaImageConverter then refused the 8-vs-10 bit mismatch.
AOSP's own software decoders (C2SoftGav1Dec) avoid exactly this by
declaring C2StreamPixelFormatInfo::output and down-converting when the
client did not ask for P010 (e.g. MediaMetadataRetriever HDR10
thumbnails); this component never gave CCodec that parameter to
configure.

Fix, following the C2SoftGav1Dec pattern:
- DecodeInterface: declare C2StreamPixelFormatInfo::output
  (YCBCR_420_888 default, IMPLEMENTATION_DEFINED always allowed to
  detect surface mode, YCBCR_P010 added only when the codec has a
  10-bit profile in caps.supportedProfiles);
- V4L2DecodeComponent/V4L2Decoder: read the client-negotiated pixel
  format at component start; on DRC, if the V4L2 capture format is
  10-bit (P010/MTST/MTSR) but the client asked for plain
  YCBCR_420_888 (ByteBuffer, not surface, not P010), allocate an 8-bit
  NV12 block instead of P010, and detile into it instead of failing;
- VideoFrame::detileFrom10To8 / LibyuvImageConverter::convertMT10ToNV12:
  detile the MT10 tiled 10-bit capture buffer straight into 8-bit NV12
  by truncating each sample to its high 8 bits during the existing
  untile pass, instead of untiling to P010 and then running a separate
  libyuv down-convert pass over the whole frame again: libyuv has no
  public API for MediaTek's MT10 tiling anyway, and the untile already
  computes every 10-bit sample once.
- surface mode (IMPLEMENTATION_DEFINED) and genuinely 8-bit streams
  take neither new code path: the switch to NV12 only fires for a
  10-bit V4L2 capture format together with an explicit 8-bit client
  request.

Test (device-free change; on-device run is for the main thread):
CodecRig decode hevc_main10_10bit_1080p30.mp4 c2.v4l2.hevc.decoder
buffer, expect 150/150 frames decoded with no realloc failure; compare
frame hashes against CodecRig decode hevc_main10_10bit_1080p30.mp4
c2.android.hevc.decoder buffer, since the previously-working P010
surface output is not directly comparable to an 8-bit ByteBuffer and
the AOSP software HEVC decoder already performs the same 10-to-8-bit
downconversion for ByteBuffer clients. Not run from here (device-free);
surface-mode HEVC10 and 8-bit HEVC/AVC ByteBuffer decode are unchanged
by construction and should be spot-checked too.

Risk: only the new combination (10-bit V4L2 capture format + explicit
8-bit client request) takes a new path; every other combination keeps
the exact pixel format and detile call it used before this change. The
new C2StreamPixelFormatInfo parameter is additive; a client that never
queries or sets it keeps getting the previous default behavior.
